(1) Lemma 3.3(3): compliers pay c=0.3; evaders face s(x) = q0 (1 - x) F, falling with the evader share x
   q0 F = 0.48 (s(0)) vs c=0.3: x0=0.05: x_T=0.000; x0=0.5: x_T=1.000; x0=0.95: x_T=1.000  -> bistable, tipping share 0.375
   q0 F = 1.20 (s(0)) vs c=0.3: x0=0.05: x_T=0.000; x0=0.5: x_T=0.000; x0=0.95: x_T=1.000  -> bistable, tipping share 0.750
   q0 F = 0.24 (s(0)) vs c=0.3: x0=0.05: x_T=1.000; x0=0.5: x_T=1.000; x0=0.95: x_T=1.000  -> evasion is globally stable (s(0) < c)
   with q0 F = 1.2 the sanction falls with x but stays above c until x = 0.75; still bistable. A sanction that stays above c for ALL x
   (s(x) = 0.5 + 0.5(1-x) >= 0.5 > 0.3): from x0=0.95 the evader share goes to 0.000: compliance globally stable, no tipping share.
   -> the lemma's 'both stable' clause needs the premise s(0) > c > s(1).
(2) Prop 5.4 with two deviations: A has (g=1.0, p_d=0.1), B has (g=0.2, p_d=0.6); C=3, D=6, v=1, delta=0.9
   deviation A (max g): condition holds = True; deviation B (smaller g, larger p_d): holds = False
   -> the statement must quantify over deviations (each with its own p_d), or assume p_d common to all deviations.
